[include version_include.man]
[manpage_begin ns_getform n [vset version]]
[moddesc {NaviServer Built-in Commands}]

[titledesc {Return an ns_set that contains all of the query data that was part of the HTTP request}]

[description]

This command returns a handle to an [term ns_set] which contains all
of the query data from the current HTTP request.  The resulting data
includes query parameters passed in the URL as well as multipart form
data.  If there is no form data, an empty [term ns_set] is created.

[section {COMMANDS}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ns_getform ] \
    [opt [option "-fallbackcharset [arg charset]"]] \
    [opt [arg charset]] \
]

 This command returns a handle to an [term ns_set] containing form data
 from the current HTTP request. It selects either the request body or
 the URL query parameters; it does not merge the two sources. If the
 selected source contains no data, an empty [term ns_set] is returned.

[para]
 Supported content types include
 [term application/x-www-form-urlencoded], [term multipart/form-data],
 and JSON media types.
 See [cmd "ns_conn form"] for the content-type selection rules, including
 the compatibility exception that requests with a JSON content type and
 a zero-length body use the URL query parameters.

[para]
 To access URL query parameters independently of the request content type
 and body, use:

[example_begin]
 set query [lb]ns_parsequery [lb]ns_conn query[rb][rb]
[example_end]

 This also works when both URL query parameters and a request body are
 supplied. The returned set contains only the URL query parameters.

[para]
 In addition to the parsing performed by [cmd "ns_conn form"],
 [cmd ns_getform] handles uploaded files. For each uploaded file field,
 two additional entries are created in the [term ns_set], named
 input_name[term .content-type] and input_name[term .tmpfile].
 The latter contains the name of a temporary file holding the uploaded
 content. The temporary file is deleted automatically when the
 connection is closed.

[para]
 For URL query parameters and URL-encoded or multipart form data,
 decoding errors can raise an exception with error code
 [const NS_INVALID_UTF8]. The fallback charset specifies an alternative
 character set to try when the initial decoding produces invalid UTF-8.
 It can be configured through the global or per-server
 [term formfallbackcharset] parameter. A warning is logged when invalid
 UTF-8 is encountered.

[para]
 The option [option "-fallbackcharset [arg charset]"] specifies the
 fallback charset explicitly, overriding the configuration setting.

[example_begin]
 ns_getform -fallbackcharset windows-1252
[example_end]

[para]
 The positional [arg charset] argument sets the connection's URL
 charset, overriding the default established by [term urlcharset].
 This charset is used for URL query parameters and as the default for
 URL-encoded or multipart form data when no charset is specified.
 It differs from the fallback charset, which is used only after a
 decoding failure.

[example_begin]
 ns_getform iso8859-1
[example_end]

[para]
 JSON body decoding uses the charset specified in the content-type
 header, defaulting to UTF-8. The positional [arg charset] argument
 and [option -fallbackcharset] do not control JSON body decoding.
[list_end]

[keywords form urlcharset formfallbackcharset content-type]
[see_also ns_getcontent ns_getformfile ns_queryget ns_set ns_mktemp \
        ns_conn ns_atclose ns_parsequery]

[manpage_end]
